<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Drikung Kagyu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1179–</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Drikung <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagyu">Kagyu</a> is a school of the Kagyu tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>, founded in 1179 by Jigten Sumgön at Drikung Thil monastery north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>. In the 13th century the Drikung sought Mongol patronage in rivalry with the Sakya, and their revolt was crushed by Yuan and Sakya forces in 1290, when Drikung monastery was burned. The school survived and remains active today.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/drikung-kagyu.jpg" alt="Drikung Kagyu" width="320" height="498" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Drikung_Kagyu_Lineage_Tree.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Drikung Kagyü is one of the eight minor lineages of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagyu">Kagyu school</a>, so called because they were founded by disciples of Phagmo Drupa (1110-1170), the main disciple of Gampopa (1079-1153), rather than by Gampopa's own immediate disciples. Like all Kagyu lineages it traces its origins to the Indian master Tilopa and to Naropa. According to an account of the time, the teachings of its founder, Jigten Sumgön (1143-1217) of the Kyura clan, drew more than 100,000 people at once, and at most 130,000.</p><p>Its sub-schools included the Lhapa or Lhanangpa Kagyü, founded by Nö Lhanangpa (1164–1224), who came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhutan">Bhutan</a> in 1194. The Lhapa were once important in western Bhutan, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thimphu">Thimphu</a> and Paro, as rivals of the Drukpa Kagyü, and came into conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngawang-namgyal">Ngawang Namgyal</a> (1594–1651). In 1640 their remaining followers were expelled from Bhutan for siding with the Tsangpa invaders against the Drukpa and refusing to acknowledge the authority of the Shabdrung.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Jigten Sumgön's doctrines are preserved in the Single Intention, or Gongchig, and The Essence of Mahāyāna Teachings. The school's main practices are the Five-fold Profound Path of Mahamudrā, which runs from arousing bodhichitta through deity yoga, guru yoga and Mahamudrā meditation to the dedication of merit, and the Six Dharmas of Nāropa, traditionally cultivated together in retreat after the preliminary practices called ngondro. Its main deity is Chakrasamvara, in union with his consort Vajravarahi.</p><p>The lineage is best known for its development of Phowa, one of the Six Yogas of Naropa, in which the practitioner learns to expel the consciousness through the top of the skull at the moment of death; the practice is said to help one remain aware through death and attain enlightenment in the Bardo. Its female protector, Achi Chokyi Drolma, the great-grandmother of Jigten Sumgön, is held to have prophesied his birth and vowed to protect his lineage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Drikung <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagyu">Kagyu</a> is one of four Kagyu lineages that still exist as independent institutions. Besides the Drikung Valley in Ü it has a strong presence in Nangqên County, in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> including Kailash, and in Ladakh, where its main monasteries are Phyang and Lamayuru, with roughly 50 more. Its headquarters in exile is Jangchubling at Dehra Dun in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttarakhand">Uttarakhand</a>, and Drikung centres in the West were begun in 1978. In 2002 the American Khenmo Drolma became the lineage's first fully ordained nun.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Drikung Kagyu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rivalry with other monastic orders was intense, and a revolt by the <b>Drikung</b> was crushed with Mongol troops in 1290.</blockquote><a href="/state/sakya-regime">Sakya regime</a></li></ul></section>
